 bit of liquid in the bucket and  then it gradually mostly leaked out it  was probably some residue in there a few  drops but you probably lost a lot of the  meat of what you had learned so it's one  one example you if you've ever been a  couple years into studying a language  like a year or two and then you took a  few weeks off you probably noticed that  your level had diminished a bit probably  it had gone down but you probably didn't  feel like you would lost most of what  you'd learned because it's that gradual  diminishment is that gradual lowering of  the level it's a slow leak so the more  of a beginner you are the more of your  ability you will lose if you're not  consistent but the way to always  maintain your level and to keep  increasing it is to be consistent and  top up the bucket every day so that's  one way I like to think about your  consistency and studying the other way I  like to think about it is in terms of  investing when you invest money over  time when you do it consistently you can  gain compound interest right that means  that you make interest on the money that  you invest but then you make interest on  the interest as well this is similar to  a phenomena that I've noticed when  studying consistently when you study  everyday consistently and you don't take  days off you're learning kind of takes  on a life of its own and you end up  learning more than you would expect from  the amount you're studying right that's  partly because your brain is learning in  the off time when you're not studying  it's kind of processing things in the  background all day but basically if you  are consistent then you can get your  you're learning to a sort of critical  mass where it starts to take on a life  of its own you learn more than you would  have if you had studied that same number  of days but with breaks in between so  it's really important to to always keep  investing on a daily basis  investing regularly so that you can get  that compounding effect